<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kargil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kargil War was an armed conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> from May to July 1999 in the Kargil district, then part of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Pakistani soldiers and militants had occupied high positions on the Indian side of the Line of Control; the Indian army and air force recaptured most of them in hard mountain fighting, and under US pressure Pakistan withdrew. It was the first war between the two states after both had tested nuclear weapons.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">Partition of India</a> in 1947 Kargil was a tehsil of Ladakh. The Indo-Pakistani War of 1947–1948 ended with the Line of Control dividing Ladakh district, and after the war of 1971 the two states signed the Simla Agreement promising not to engage in armed conflict over that boundary. In the 1990s separatist activity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and the nuclear tests that both countries conducted in 1998 made the atmosphere increasingly belligerent. In February 1999 the two governments signed the Lahore Declaration, promising a peaceful bilateral solution to the Kashmir dispute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>During February 1999 the Pakistan Army sent forces to occupy posts on the Indian side of the Line of Control. Special Services Group troops and four to seven battalions of the Northern Light Infantry, a paramilitary regiment, set up bases on 132 vantage points. Pakistan at first blamed independent Kashmiri insurgents, but documents left by casualties and later statements by its Prime Minister and army chief showed the involvement of its paramilitary forces. By the second week of May an ambush of a patrol led by Captain Saurabh Kalia, who had acted on a shepherd's tip-off, exposed the intrusion. Indian estimates of the number of infiltrators rose from some militants on 12 May to over 1,000 by 21 May.</p><p>The task of repelling them fell to the 15th Corps. In the Batalik sector an assault on Point 5203 on 6 June led to heavy casualties, and the point was captured on 21 June. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>, later supported by the Indian Air Force, recaptured a majority of the positions, while the Indian Navy prepared to blockade Pakistani ports in Operation Talwar. President Bill Clinton refused to intervene until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> removed all its forces from the Indian side. After the Washington accord of 4 July 1999, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nawaz-sharif">Nawaz Sharif</a> agreed to withdraw Pakistani troops, most fighting stopped. On 27 July Pakistani forces asked for a flag meeting to collect their dead and agreed to abide by the ceasefire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> was heavily criticised abroad for instigating the war, and its claim that Kashmiri freedom fighters were responsible failed. Two soldiers received the Nishan-E-Haider, its highest military honour, which confirmed the army's role. Pakistani casualties are hard to determine: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pervez-musharraf">Pervez Musharraf</a> stated that 357 soldiers were killed, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nawaz-sharif">Nawaz Sharif</a> later said over 4,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> awarded four Param Vir Chakras and eleven Maha Vir Chakras. The Kargil Review Committee under K. Subrahmanyam led to a large-scale restructuring of Indian intelligence. The Kargil War was one of only two major conventional wars between nuclear-armed states, the other being the Sino-Soviet border conflict.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kargil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Four conventional wars have been fought with India: the Kashmir war of 1947–1948, which left Pakistan a third of the region; 1965; 1971, when India's support for the Bengali Mukti Bahini ended in Indian victory and the independence of Bangladesh; and the <b>Kargil War</b> of 1999.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1999 the <b>Kargil War</b> drove Pakistani forces from heights in Kashmir.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Military</a></li></ul></section>
